TAIL(1)                        P32W Unix                         TAIL(1)

NAME
       tail - print the last lines of a file

SYNOPSIS
       tail [-n [+]count | -count | +count] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
       tail copies the last count lines (default 10) of each file,
       or of the standard input if no file is named (or for a file
       named -), to the standard output. It reads the whole input,
       keeping only the last count lines in memory. Lines may be of
       any length.

       With more than one file, each file's lines are preceded by a
       header line ==> name <==, and the headers after the first by
       an empty line.

OPTIONS
       -n count
       -count print the last count lines instead of 10, e.g. -n 20
              or -20 (also -n20); 0 prints nothing
       -n +count
       +count start at line count (1 is the first) and copy the
              rest, e.g. +2 leaves out the first line

       A count that is not a number is a usage error.

EXAMPLES
       tail /usr/ken/log             the last ten lines
       tail -1 /etc/passwd           the newest account
       ps | tail -3
       tail +2 table                 all but the heading line

EXIT STATUS
       0; 1 for a usage error or if a file could not be opened
       (tail says why and goes on with the next).

SEE ALSO
       head(1), cat(1), more(1)

BUGS
       There is no -f (follow a growing file), no -c (bytes) and no
       -r. A count of 0 prints nothing and does not read the input.
       A file whose last line has no newline runs into the next
       file's header.
